<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Congo War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1998">1998</a>–<a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Africa's World War, Great War of Africa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Congo War was a conflict in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> that began on 2 August 1998, after President Laurent-Désiré Kabila turned against the Rwandan and Ugandan allies who had helped him seize power. It drew in nine African nations and about 25 armed groups. Rwandan troops and RCD rebels overran the Kivus, while Zimbabwean and Angolan forces saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusaka">Lusaka</a> Peace Agreement of 1999 and Kabila's assassination in January 2001, the war officially ended on 18 July 2003, though violence persisted in the east. A 2008 report by the International Rescue Committee estimated 5.4 million deaths, mainly from disease and malnutrition.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war had its roots in the democratisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> from 1990 and in conflicts involving the Banyarwanda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinyarwanda">Kinyarwanda</a> speakers who had migrated in waves to the Kivu region. Their nationality remained unclear, and confrontations with the Hunde, Nande and Nyanga took place in 1991 and 1992 over citizenship, land and customary authority. The First Congo War began in 1996 with the Banyamulenge rebellion and brought Laurent-Désiré Kabila to power. Rwandan and Burundian troops stayed in the east, which increased anti-Tutsi feeling. Amid rumours of a Rwandan-backed coup plot, Kabila ordered all Rwandan troops to leave on 27 July 1998.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 2 August 1998 Sylvain Mbuki, commander of the 10th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a>, announced over a radio station in Goma that the military had decided to remove Kabila. With Rwandan support the rebels took Goma, Bukavu and Uvira, and on 12 August the Rally for Congolese Democracy was formed. An attempt to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a> in August was stopped by Zimbabwean and Angolan troops. Rwandan forces and the rebels then overran North and South Kivu and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisangani">Kisangani</a> on 23 August, which brought in Uganda.</p><p>The Lusaka Peace Agreement of July and August 1999 largely stopped movement on the front lines, but fighting continued along a front of 2,400 kilometres. On 16 January 2001 Laurent-Désiré Kabila was shot dead in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a>, most probably by a bodyguard, and his successor Joseph Kabila revived the peace process. The war officially ended on 18 July 2003 with a transitional government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The war caused widespread sexual violence and the conscription of children, and the International Criminal Court began an investigation in 2004. Travel overland became impossible and internal trade came to a halt, and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> there was no ship traffic between regions held by different powers for years. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a> food prices quadrupled within a year. Violence continued in the east after 2003, in conflicts such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lords-resistance-army">Lord's Resistance Army</a> insurgency and the Kivu and Ituri conflicts, and trade in minerals helped fund the fighting.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Congo War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…byword for the failure of the international order, and reshaped the Great Lakes: the International Criminal Tribunal for Rwanda was created to try its organisers, and the flight of two million Hutu into Zaïre and the RPF army's pursuit helped set off the First and <b>Second Congo Wars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Congo War</b> began in 1998; the most widely reported estimate of its death toll is 5.4 million people, though other estimates put it at 3 million.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li></ul></section>
